  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nToday (Thursday) will again bring summer-like high temperatures\nto the Midsouth, but with lower humidity. Late Wednesday\nafternoon surface observations showed mid 40s dewpoints over much\nof central MS around Jackson, while lower to mid 70s dewpoints\nprevailed over most of the Midsouth. JAN upper sounding from 12Z\nWednesday showed an average 925-850mb dewpoint near 0C. This\nlayer won't be quite as dry over the Midsouth today, but we'll\nnonetheless see surface dewpoints about 10 degrees cooler than\nWednesday.\n\nThe main weather event remains a strong cold frontal passage\nFriday morning, aided by a deep northern branch longwave trough\nrotating through the Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley. 18Z GFS\ndepicted stratospheric vorticity incorporated into this trough,\nwith the dynamic tropospheric pressure level down to 600mb over\nthe Ohio River Valley. Quite a dynamic system and a sign of the\nchanging seasons.\n\nAs the upper trough lifts off the Mid-Atlantic seaboard Saturday,\na progressive upper level ridge will build through the southern\nand central Great Plains, with weakening northwest flow over the\nMidsouth. This ridge will expand through much of the southeast US\non Sunday, as it deamplifies. The net result for the Midsouth\nwill be diminishing surface winds by Sunday morning, allowing the\ndry air to cool to the upper 30s and lower 40s toward sunrise.\n\nThe dry airmass will modify from Sunday onward, with highs\nreaching normal by Tuesday. Medium range global models depict the\nupper level ridge amplifying over the latter half of the week,\ndownstream of a closed low moving onshore into southern\nCalifornia. There are some disparity between the ECMWF and GEFS\nensemble means with respect to timing of the upstream Pacific\nlow. This timing may be sorted out in the subsequent runs, but\nfor now the overall trend of warmer temps late next week appears\nto be a good bet.",
